      "text": "# Playbook: funding and finance routes\n\nOwner interview, rounds 1-2 (2026-11-09). This is how a seasoned public-affairs\npractitioner works a public-funding or public-finance case. The agent follows\nit; it does not improvise around it. Status: DRAFT, awaiting the owner's corrections.\n\n## 1. Before any instrument: establish the structure\nNever name an instrument before these are known (ask only what the company\nprofile lacks):\n- **Legal structure:** legal form, where incorporated, where it has branches,\n  subsidiaries or establishments, and in which markets it operates or wants to.\n  Eligibility is decided by structure first: a fund open to \"an SME established\n  in Bavaria\" is closed to a company with no Bavarian establishment, however\n  good the project.\n- **Regulatory status:** licences held or not held (banking, payment,\n  investment firm, intermediary registrations). See the deal-breakers in §4.\n- **Alignment:** how the company's activity fits the fund's goals and the\n  relevant industrial agenda (national, regional, EU). Alignment is not only an\n  eligibility test, it is a **goodwill lever**: a case framed in the\n  institution's own priorities is read differently.\n- **Documents:** what each vehicle's process will demand. Preparing them is part\n  of the advice, not an afterthought.\n\n## 2. The working order\n1. **Map the structure and gather the foundational facts** (§1). Advance\n   informed or not at all.\n2. **Map the institutions** in every market the company wants to reach, at\n   every level that matters: **regional, local, national, EU**.\n3. **Within each institution, rank the people:** priority-1 and priority-2\n   contacts, i.e. those you need to win over to create goodwill.\n4. **Targeted outreach** (§3).\n\n## 2b. Two kinds of stakeholder, and who is priority 1\nPublic financing always has both:\n- **Political stakeholders** move agendas, forward you to the right people and\n  put you on top of the pile. They open doors; they do not sign.\n- **Public-capital stakeholders** are operational: the people who grant,\n  guarantee, invest and sign off.\nBoth matter; work them together.\n\n**Priority 1** are the people responsible for the matter, high enough that\nlanding on their desk produces a top-down hand-off: directors-general, heads\nof unit, and the head of the specific instrument or line (for a portfolio\nguarantee: head of guarantees / microfinance / alternative lending). Not the\nCEO or the board of a KfW: one level down from the very top.\n**Priority 2** are the next level down: heads of the specific division or\ninstrument a P1 would forward you to.\n- If P1 does not answer, go down to the heads of unit.\n- With a genuine personal contact, you can escalate to the board.\n- A top-down introduction (\"the director asked me to look at this\") is worth\n  more than any cold approach lower down.\n\n## 3. Outreach that gets read\n- **Research the person first:** who they are, where they have been, what they\n  care about, which regional or ministerial agenda they carry. Tailor every\n  message to that agenda.\n- **Reach them in the right function.** The same person can hold several roles\n  (a Bundestag member who is also a ministry official). Write to the role that\n  decides your matter, through that role's channel, in that role's language.\n  Writing to the wrong function is a classic junior mistake.\n- **Formal, local, strategic.** Formal register, local context, respect for the\n  position. Senior people (P1, P2) speak strategy, not operations: the case is\n  \"this is a strategic priority for you\", and eligibility through structure\n  is justified *later*. Example opener for Germany: the CEO is German, the\n  company is a European champion aligned with industrial robotics deployment,\n  and it helps the Mittelstand get the financing it needs to modernise and\n  revive the German industrial agenda. Speak the language they want to hear.\n- **No AI voice.** Nothing weak, vague or long. Cut to the bone and show you\n  know the field, without operational detail.\n- **Bring something to the table.** Introduce the company in two lines, give\n  just enough context, state why it serves *their* priority, and ask for a\n  meeting to discuss alignment and opportunities (e.g. regional priorities,\n  collaboration).\n- **Short and concise, always.** The paper-stack test: picture a German\n  Landtag or Bundestag member who finds a stack of copied papers on the desk\n  every morning and allocates them through the day to the right ministry or\n  head of unit. Your message must stand out on that stack and be routable in\n  one read. Institutions work old-school; write for that.\n\n## 3b. Knowing the agenda: be present on every channel\nBeing informed is the alpha and omega. Read every channel an institution or\nregion speaks through: media, social media, newsletters, press releases,\nspeeches, strategies. Example: before approaching Bavaria on robotics, know\nBavaria's robotics strategy. The agent maintains this per institution and\nregion, and the radar watches it.\n\n## 4. Deal-breakers: where institutional flexibility is near zero\nScan for these before recommending anything. They are formal; no amount of\ngoodwill moves them.\n- **Formal eligibility of the applicant type.** Example, guarantees for a\n  lender's loan portfolio:\n  - **Germany:** the state guarantees SME lending through the normal banks\n    (the **Hausbank** principle, KfW and the Bürgschaftsbanken). An\n    alternative lender without a banking licence is generally not the\n    channel; the route runs through a house bank.\n  - **Italy:** authorised financial-intermediary status under **Art. 106 TUB**\n    is what opens national guarantee schemes (Fondo di Garanzia per le PMI)\n    more easily.\n  - **France:** different mechanics. NOT asserted from instinct: the agent\n    must research and cite the formal documents (Bpifrance guarantee\n    products, Code monétaire et financier) before advising.\n  - **EU / InvestEU (EIF):** more open to alternative lenders as financial\n    intermediaries. **Watch:** InvestEU is moving under the European\n    Competitiveness Fund; check whether alternative lenders stay eligible\n    (carve-outs) as the rules are rewritten.\n- **Sequencing.** A mentality, not a list: *before every move, ask what must\n  already be done for the next step to remain possible.* Examples: a\n  lobby/transparency-register entry before engaging officials; export-credit\n  or market-entry support usually requires the application (and the budget)\n  to be filed **before** entering the market; starting work before applying\n  can void an aid's incentive effect. Never be in a situation where a\n  requirement was not anticipated.\n- **Processes.** Formal channels, portals, deadlines, required documents.\n\n## 4b. Keep a finger on the pulse\nRules change under you: programmes merge, mandates move, carve-outs appear or\nvanish (InvestEU into the European Competitiveness Fund is the live example).\nEvery route the agent recommends is also put on the workspace watchlist, so a\nrule change that reopens or closes it is flagged.\n\n## 4c. Prioritise, probe, park; rarely walk away\n- **Rarely cut ties.** Only an explicit, hard no ends a route; otherwise park\n  it and monitor whether the rules change.\n- **Spend energy where the return is highest.** Do not pursue every route\n  equally. Pick a few public routes to run **in parallel**, because public\n  processes are slow (unlike a VC partner putting EUR 200k on the table).\n- **Low-effort probes are worth it.** A short, very specific message to the\n  head of guarantees or investments at a public institution, asking what the\n  options are, costs little. If they cannot accommodate the need: park it,\n  monitor, move on.\n\n## 5. Where juniors go wrong\n- The **preparatory steps**: not asking the right questions up front, so the\n  real blockers surface late.\n- **Outreach to the wrong function** (§3).\n- Messages that are too long, without context, or that bring nothing.\n\n## Open\nThe owner's model answers for the eval cases; corrections to this draft.\n"
